{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-33068",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨3+2t,2t,1+t⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨3+2t,2t,1+t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨2,-1,4⟩ & =4 \\\\ ⟨3+2t,2t,1+t⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-1,4⟩ & =4 \\\\ 2(3+2t)-2t+4(1+t) & =4 \\\\ 6+4t-2t+4+4t & =4 \\\\ 6t & =-6 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$ is obtained by substituting $t=-1$ into the equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨3+2(-1),2(-1),1+(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(1,-2,0).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
